{"rulings":[{"amendment_id":"fr-7-e6495363","decision":"reject","rationale":"The amendment’s premise does not hold in the current worktree. `git ls-files --stage -- crates/tank_core/tests/nitrification_alkalinity.rs` returns an indexed entry for that path, `git status --short -- crates/tank_core/tests/nitrification_alkalinity.rs` is clean, and `git log -- crates/tank_core/tests/nitrification_alkalinity.rs` shows prior commits containing the file. That means the test file is already tracked and not missing from the patch."},{"amendment_id":"fr-7-43506246","decision":"reject","rationale":"The cited defect does not exist in the present code. [`crates/tank_core/src/budget_helpers.rs`] explicitly documents at lines 67-68 that non-mg diagnostics such as alkalinity remain on `BudgetMetric`, and `Element` only contains `Nitrogen`, `Carbon`, and `Oxygen`. The cited assertion paths around lines 473 and 495 are still mg-only N/C/O budget messages; there is no `Element::Alkalinity` branch there. Because the current worktree has no alkalinity-through-`Element` path, the hard-coded `mg` labels are not presently mislabeling alkalinity diagnostics."}],"summary":"Both disputed amendments are rejected. The current worktree shows `crates/tank_core/tests/nitrification_alkalinity.rs` is already tracked and clean, and `budget_helpers.rs` still limits `Element` to the mg-based N/C/O ledger with alkalinity handled separately via `BudgetMetric`, so the asserted alkalinity unit-label bug is not present."}